tel 1.0

base gigantism
import soundness

info   metaprogramming helpers: every given instance in scope, macro result types, imports at a call site
detail
    _Gigantism_ is a small toolkit for the Soundness libraries' own macros, and for any
    library which, like them, needs a little more from the compiler than Scala's public
    reflection API offers.

    Its central feature is `every[T]`, which collects *every* given instance of `T` visible
    where it is called — including instances which are mutually ambiguous, which an ordinary
    `summon` rejects — into an `Every[T]`. This is how a library lets a user enable several
    options at once by importing several givens: the `Boolean` decoder collects
    `Every[Truth]`, and coaxial's sockets collect `Every` socket option in scope.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Colour(name: Text)

    object Colours:
      given red: Colour = Colour(t"red")
      given green: Colour = Colour(t"green")
      given blue: Colour = Colour(t"blue")

    import Colours.{red, blue}
    every[Colour].values.map(_.name)   // red and blue, in either order
    ```

    Alongside it are `Macro[T]`, the type of a macro implementation's result; `Lifts`, which
    lifts Soundness `List`s of expressions into quoted lists and varargs; and
    `metaprogramming.imports`, which lists the imports in scope at a macro's call site.

    `every` and `metaprogramming` reach into the compiler's internal API
    (`dotty.tools.dotc`), so they are tied to the compiler version Soundness is built with.
    The library has a single source set, `gigantism.core`, and everything is exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella.

refer gigantism.Every
refer gigantism.every
refer gigantism.Macro

entry .Every   every given instance of a type visible at a call site, collected into a list
  detail
      An `Every[T]` holds all the given `T`s in scope where it was summoned, in `#values`.
      It is made by `every[T]`, or summoned as a context parameter through the companion's
      `.default` given, which needs no import: a method which takes `(using Every[Option])`
      sees every `Option` given its caller has in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Shape(name: Text)

      def shapes(using all: Every[Shape]): Int = all.values.length

      given triangle: Shape = Shape(t"triangle")
      given square: Shape = Shape(t"square")
      shapes   // 2
      ```

      Instances are found whether they are local, imported by name or in implicit scope, and
      mutually ambiguous ones are all included; an instance out of scope is not. The order of
      the values is not specified. With no instance in scope, `#values` is empty.
  refer gigantism.every
  entry .default   summons an `Every[T]` of all the `T` instances in scope, as a context parameter
      A transparent inline given: it expands at each use site, so it sees the instances in
      scope there.
  entry .summonAll   the macro which collects every instance of a type in scope at its expansion site
      The implementation behind `every` and `.default`. It repeatedly searches for an
      instance while ignoring those already found, and recovers both alternatives from an
      ambiguous search through the compiler's internal `AmbiguousImplicits`.
  entry #values   the instances found, as a standard-library `List`
      The list is the Scala standard library's, not Soundness's, because the instances
      collected may carry capabilities, which Soundness's `List` does not hold.
      `List.from(every[T].values)` converts it.

entry .every   collects every given instance of a type visible at the call site, as an `Every`
    `every[T]` expands to an `Every[T]` holding each given `T` in scope at the call,
    including instances which are ambiguous with one another. It does not fail when none is
    in scope: the result is then empty.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Shape(name: Text)

    locally:
      given local: Shape = Shape(t"triangle")
      every[Shape].values.length   // 1
    ```

entry .Macro   the type of a macro implementation's result: `Quotes ?=> Expr[T]`
    A macro implementation declared to return `Macro[T]` receives the `Quotes` as a context
    parameter, so `def impl(x: Expr[Int]): Macro[Text] = '{…}` needs no `(using Quotes)`.

entry .Lifts   lifts Soundness `List`s of expressions into quoted lists and varargs
  detail
      The counterparts of `Expr.ofList` and `Varargs` for macro code which holds its lifted
      expressions in a Soundness `List` rather than a standard-library `Seq`.
  entry .list   lifts a `List` of expressions into an expression of a `List`
  entry .varargs   lifts a `List` of expressions into a varargs argument, `Expr[Seq[T]]`
  entry .Varargs   matches a varargs argument, giving its element expressions as a `List`
    detail
        The extractor counterpart of `Lifts.varargs`: `case Lifts.Varargs(elements) =>`
        matches an `Expr[Seq[T]]` written as literal arguments.
    entry .unapply   the element expressions of a varargs argument, if it was written as literal arguments

entry .metaprogramming   compiler facilities beyond the public reflection API, for the `Quotes` in scope
    Inside a macro implementation, `metaprogramming` wraps the `Quotes` in scope as a
    `Metaprogramming`, so that `metaprogramming.imports` lists the imports visible at the
    macro's call site.

entry .Metaprogramming   compiler facilities beyond the public reflection API, for one `Quotes`
  detail
      Made with `metaprogramming` inside a macro implementation. It reaches into the
      compiler's internal context, so it depends on the version of the compiler.
  entry #Import   one import visible at a macro's call site
    entry #wildcard   whether the import is a wildcard, `import p.*`
    entry #term   the term imported from, as a `TermRef`
  entry #quotes   the `Quotes` this wraps
  entry #imports   every import in scope at the macro's call site, outermost first
